        <div id="buying-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Common Treadmill-Buying Mistakes That Cost Shoppers Later</h2>
        <p>
          We hear from a lot of readers after the fact about what they wish
          they'd checked before buying, and the same handful of mistakes come
          up again and again across every category in this roundup.
        </p>
        <h3>Ignoring Real Floor Space Until Delivery Day</h3>
        <p>
          Spec sheets list dimensions, but shoppers routinely underestimate
          how much clearance a treadmill needs on all sides, not just its
          footprint. Measure the actual room, including doorway width for
          delivery, before ordering, regardless of which category you're
          shopping in.
        </p>
        <h3>Buying Features You'll Never Use</h3>
        <p>
          A <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>40% incline</a> range or a 32-inch touchscreen is genuinely valuable
          for the right buyer and completely wasted on someone who just wants
          a reliable place to walk each evening. Match the category to your
          actual training pattern rather than the most impressive spec
          sheet.
        </p>
        <h3>Underestimating Subscription Costs</h3>
        <p>
          Several machines in this roundup, and across the market broadly,
          derive much of their advertised value from an ongoing content
          subscription. Factor that recurring cost into your total price
          comparison, not just the sticker price of the hardware itself.
        </p>
        <h3>Skipping the Weight Capacity Buffer</h3>
        <p>
          Buying right at a machine's stated weight limit, rather than
          leaving a comfortable buffer below it, is a common regret we hear
          about across every price tier, from budget folding machines to
          premium commercial-grade units.
        </p>
        <h3>Comparing Across Categories Instead of Within Them</h3>
        <p>
          It's tempting to compare a folding treadmill's price directly
          against a commercial-grade machine's price and conclude one is
          simply the better deal, but that comparison misses the point.
          These categories exist because they solve fundamentally different
          problems. A fair comparison happens within a category, folding
          treadmill against folding treadmill, budget pick against budget
          pick, not across categories that were never competing for the same
          buyer in the first place.
        </p>
        <h3>Not Planning for Delivery and Assembly</h3>
        <p>
          Heavier machines, especially in the commercial-grade and best
          overall categories, often require more than one person to safely
          assemble, and some retailers charge extra for white-glove delivery
          and setup. Factor that potential cost and logistics requirement
          into your decision before checkout rather than discovering it
          after the box arrives on your doorstep.
        </p>
        <p>
          The single biggest mistake, though, is shopping by star rating
          alone without matching the category to your actual constraints.
          Every machine in this roundup earned its badge for a specific
          reason tied to a specific kind of buyer, and the highest-rated
          treadmill overall isn't automatically the right treadmill for your
          space, budget, or goals.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
